{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>וְשָׁבְתָה הָאָרֶץ שַׁבָּת לַה׳.</b> Regarding the reason for this commandment there are divergent opinions, for many say that the reason is that the land should rest so that it may add to give its strength for sowing, and to this opinion the Moreh Nevukhim inclined. And many disagree with him and say: if the Torah was concerned about this, that the soil not weaken, why should they be liable to exile for the failure to observe the sabbatical year of the land — the punishment would merely be that the land does not add its strength for them. And furthermore, this would not be a שַׁבָּת לַה׳ but only for the land's own needs; and what is meant by 'then the land shall enjoy its sabbatical years' — what advantage is there in this, that Israel will be exiled from it and the nations will settle upon it and work it forever? How does the land rest through being worked by them? And the Akeidah gave another reason and said that the purpose of this commandment is to remember the creation of the world and to remember the days of old and the end of one's days, and thus wrote Mahari'a, and he added yet more embellishment of his own. This too is far from my grasp, for it resembles Yehudah and moreover a verse to read, for the weekly Sabbath every single week comes to remember the creation of the world — and if that does not avail, how will this, which comes at distant intervals, help us?" "And in order to settle several linguistic fine-points in the language of the parashah, I say that the reason for this commandment is to root Israel firmly in the trait of faith and trust in HaShem. For the Holy One, blessed be He, was concerned lest when they come into the land they engage in the working of the soil according to the natural order, and when they find their hands strong, they forget HaShem and remove their trust from Him, and reckon that their own power and the might of their hand produced this wealth for them, and that the world proceeds according to its accustomed way; and they will think that the land is theirs and they are its owners and none other. Therefore HaShem took them entirely out of the natural order, for in six years the way of the nations is to sow for two years and leave one year fallow so as not to deplete its strength, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said, 'Six years you shall sow your field' every single year, and I promise you to increase its strength so that it will not be depleted. And furthermore, a miracle within a miracle: after you have sown it for six years, if in the sixth year it does not deplete its strength — at the very least it will not add strength to it; and HaShem said: on the contrary, in the sixth year I will add to it so much strength that it is said, 'and I will command My blessing in the sixth year and it shall produce the yield for three years.' And if the miracle were merely that the produce should suffice for three years, that would already be a wondrous feat; but a third marvel upon all of it: the produce that grows in the sixth year, even if there is in it only the measure of food for one year like any other place, HaShem will send the blessing into their storehouses, so that one eats a morsel and is blessed in his stomach until that produce suffices for three years — as it says, 'and it shall produce the yield' with the definite article ה, when it should have said 'and it shall produce produce for three years'; rather, the definite article ה hints that that same produce which is normally produced every single year — so too shall it produce in the sixth year, and HaShem will add strength to the land until that same produce suffices for three years. And this is certainly an open and visible miracle, greater than all the rest. And through all these signs which I have placed in your hand, you shall know that the whole earth is Mine. And through this your eyes will be lifted up toward HaShem, just as we find with the daily descent of the manna — so that their eyes would be lifted up toward HaShem always and they would trust in Him always — so too the matter of the sabbatical year: that they do not work the soil in every seventh year, there is neither sowing nor harvest, and they rely on the miracle. And this is the correct and clearer reason than anything the commentators have spoken of it." "And according to this, it follows logically that they should be liable to exile for the sin of the sabbatical year, on account of the defect of faith within them — for they did not believe in HaShem and did not trust in His deliverance (Psalms 78:22), that He would perform for them such a miracle in making the produce suffice for three years; as it is written (Jeremiah 5:1): 'Run to and fro through the streets of Jerusalem, and see now and know, and seek if you can find a man, if there be any who does justice, who seeks faithfulness — and I will pardon her.' The land itself, moreover, will be greatly zealous about this, for its desire is that this merit be brought about through it, to strengthen faith in HaShem through it. And furthermore, through this they will believe that all of them are merely tenant farmers on the land and the Holy One, blessed be He, is the master of the field; and in this matter the land desires that the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself be its master and its lord, for the earth is His, may He be blessed. And when the sabbatical years are nullified — for they hold themselves as owners of the land — it is fitting that the land should be zealous about this. Therefore it is said: 'then the land shall enjoy its sabbatical years.' But regarding the nations, the land will not be zealous if they conduct themselves upon it according to the natural order, for in any case their entire conduct is according to nature and there is no faith in them. And after this introduction we shall come to the explanation of the verses, for they accord with this true intention concerning which there is no doubt."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:57.664005170Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}